Here is a simple example of a Rust application that uses the ElevenLabs.io API to read the contents of the clipboard aloud using Text-to-Speech (TTS):
```rust
use elevenlabs_api::ElevenLabs;
use clipboard::{ClipboardContext, Clipboard};
use std::env;
use std::process;

fn main() {
    // Get the API key from the environment variable
    let api_key = env::var("ELEVENLABS_API_KEY").expect("ELEVENLABS_API_KEY must be set");

    // Create an instance of the ElevenLabs API client
    let client = ElevenLabs::new(api_key);

    // Get the contents of the clipboard
    let mut ctx = ClipboardContext::new().expect("Failed to initialize clipboard context");
    let contents = ctx.get_contents().expect("Failed to get clipboard contents");

    // Create a TTS request
    let tts_request = client.create_tts_request(contents, "en-US", "mp3");

    // Send the TTS request and get the response
    let response = client.send_tts_request(tts_request).expect("Failed to send TTS request");

    // Get the audio URL from the response
    let audio_url = response.audio_url;

    // Download the audio file
    let mut response = reqwest::blocking::get(audio_url).expect("Failed to download audio file");
    let audio_file = response.bytes().expect("Failed to get audio file bytes");

    // Save the audio file to a temporary file
    let temp_file = std::env::temp_dir().join("temp.mp3");
    std::fs::write(temp_file, audio_file).expect("Failed to write audio file to disk");

    // Play the audio file
    let mut process = process::Command::new("mpg321")
        .arg(temp_file)
        .spawn()
        .expect("Failed to spawn mpg321 process");
    process.wait().expect("Failed to wait for mpg321 process");
}
```
This code uses the `elevenlabs_api` crate to interact with the ElevenLabs.io API, the `clipboard` crate to get the contents of the clipboard, and the `reqwest` crate to download the audio file. It also uses the `mpg321` command-line tool to play the audio file.

You'll need to add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:
```toml
[dependencies]
elevenlabs_api = "0.1.0"
clipboard = "0.7.0"
reqwest = "0.11.6"
```
You'll also need to set the `ELEVENLABS_API_KEY` environment variable to your ElevenLabs.io API key.

Note that this is just a simple example, and you may want to add error handling and other features to your application. You can also use other TTS engines or audio players if you prefer. Let me know if you have any questions or need further assistance! 

