1.  **Install the necessary libraries**: You will need to install the LiquidCrystal I2C library to control the LCD display. You can do this by going to Tools > Manage Libraries in the Arduino IDE, searching for "LiquidCrystal I2C", and installing the library by Frank de Brabander [[2](https://arduinogetstarted.com/tutorials/arduino-display-temperature-from-lm35-sensor-on-lcd)].
2.  **Connect the components**: Connect the temperature sensor (LM35) to the Arduino board via VCC and GND pins. Connect the analog output pin of the LM35 to the analog input pin A0 of the Arduino. Connect the potentiometer to the LCD display, with the first pin connected to +VCC and the second pin connected to pin 3 of the LCD. The 10 K Ohm potentiometer controls the brightness of the panel [[3](https://www.hackatronic.com/arduino-with-temperature-sensor-interfacing-lcd-and-lm35/)].
